Subject: [PATCH] winewayland: give owned layered popups the subsurface role

winex11.drv and winewayland.drv carry the same rule, and upstream's patch 0005
only fixes the X11 copy:

    if (style & WS_POPUP)
    {
        /* popup with sysmenu == caption are managed */
        if (style & WS_SYSMENU) return TRUE;

JUCE menus carry WS_POPUP|WS_SYSMENU, so is_window_managed() claims them. On
Wayland a managed window becomes an xdg_toplevel, and a toplevel cannot ask to
be placed -- the protocol has no way to express "put this under the File menu".
The compositor places it by its own policy instead. Measured with rekordbox
7.2.18 (JUCE 8) on niri 25.08: every dropdown opened centred on the screen,
nowhere near the menu bar it belongs to, and each one arrived with four 14px
drop-shadow slivers (1x1, 234x9, 9x90, 9x90) as separate toplevels alongside
it. niri focuses new windows by default, so focus landed on a 1px shadow and
the application stopped accepting input.

An unmanaged window takes the other branch in WAYLAND_WindowPosChanged:

    case WAYLAND_SURFACE_ROLE_SUBSURFACE:
        wayland_surface_make_subsurface(surface, owner_surface);

which is a wl_subsurface of the owner, positioned at an exact offset from it
and never seen by the compositor as a window at all. That is what menus,
tooltips and drop shadows want on Wayland, and it is the same conclusion patch
0005 reached for X11: these are application chrome, not application windows,
and Windows never hands them to a window manager either.

NOT INVENTED HERE. The predicate and its placement are taken from
lacamar/wine-arm64ec-rpm, wine/2026_09_15-winewayland-popup-subsurfaces.patch,
which solves the same problem. Two details in it are load-bearing and both were
wrong in this patch before that was found:

  - The test must sit ABOVE "activated windows are managed". Menus are
    frequently shown activated, and that test returns TRUE before any
    style-based check below it could run.
  - GW_OWNER must be present. The subsurface role is created against the
    owner's surface, so an ownerless window returning FALSE here ends up with
    no role at all and renders nothing. proton-ge-custom carries a patch
    specifically for that case ("winewayland.drv: Avoid mapping ownerless menu
    popups as toplevels"), so it is a real hazard, not a hypothetical one.

Only the is_window_managed() hunk is taken. That patch also reworks subsurface
restacking, adds a Decorated=Y option and clamps toplevels on-screen; none of
that is needed here.

WS_EX_LAYERED alone is the discriminator rather than TOOLWINDOW|LAYERED --
0005's reproducer shows JUCE's popups carry both, so LAYERED is sufficient, and
the extra WS_EX_APPWINDOW / WS_CAPTION / WS_THICKFRAME exclusions keep real
windows that happen to be layered out of it.

ex_style is read earlier than before because the new test needs it.

---
--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/window.c
@@ -376,6 +376,40 @@
     /* child windows are not managed */
     style = NtUserGetWindowLongW(hwnd, GWL_STYLE);
     if ((style & (WS_CHILD|WS_POPUP)) == WS_CHILD) return FALSE;
+    /* RBW-POPUP-WL: menus, tooltips and drop shadows are chrome, not
+     * application windows. As an xdg_toplevel they cannot be positioned --
+     * Wayland has no protocol for a client to place a toplevel -- so the
+     * compositor puts them where its own policy says, which for a dropdown
+     * means nowhere near the menu that opened it.
+     *
+     * Measured with rekordbox 7.2.18 (JUCE 8) on niri 25.08: every dropdown
+     * opened centred on the screen, each with four 14px drop-shadow slivers
+     * as separate toplevels, and focus landing on a 1px shadow left the
+     * application unable to accept input.
+     *
+     * Returning FALSE gives them the subsurface role instead, placed at an
+     * exact offset from their owner and never seen by the compositor as a
+     * window. This mirrors patch 0005, which fixes the identical rule in
+     * winex11.drv; see it for the reproducer that isolated the styles.
+     *
+     * Two things here are load-bearing and were taken from the same fix in
+     * lacamar/wine-arm64ec-rpm (2026_09_15-winewayland-popup-subsurfaces):
+     *
+     *  - It must sit ABOVE the activation test below. Menus are frequently
+     *    shown activated, and that test returns TRUE before any style check
+     *    could run.
+     *  - GW_OWNER must exist. The subsurface role is created against the
+     *    owner's surface, so an ownerless window returning FALSE here gets
+     *    no role at all and renders nothing. */
+    ex_style = NtUserGetWindowLongW(hwnd, GWL_EXSTYLE);
+    if ((ex_style & WS_EX_LAYERED) && !(ex_style & WS_EX_APPWINDOW) &&
+        (style & WS_CAPTION) != WS_CAPTION && !(style & WS_THICKFRAME) &&
+        NtUserGetWindowRelative(hwnd, GW_OWNER))
+    {
+        TRACE("RBW-POPUP-WL %p: owned layered popup, not managed (style %#x ex %#x)\n",
+              hwnd, (int)style, (int)ex_style);
+        return FALSE;
+    }
     /* activated windows are managed */
     if (!(swp_flags & (SWP_NOACTIVATE|SWP_HIDEWINDOW))) return TRUE;
     if (hwnd == get_active_window()) return TRUE;
@@ -391,7 +425,6 @@
         if (fullscreen) return TRUE;
     }
     /* application windows are managed */
-    ex_style = NtUserGetWindowLongW(hwnd, GWL_EXSTYLE);
     if (ex_style & WS_EX_APPWINDOW) return TRUE;
     /* windows that own popups are managed */
     if (has_owned_popups(hwnd)) return TRUE;
